\details{
The bootstrap runs in the engine (ferx-core's \code{ferx-tools} crate); this
function is a thin wrapper. The percentile estimator, the exclusion filters,
the parameter naming and the \code{sample_size} syntax all live there, so an R
session and the \verb{ferx bootstrap} CLI produce the same numbers from the same
seed.
\subsection{What the bootstrap buys over the covariance step}{

ferx's standard errors are the asymptotic \code{R^-1} matrix, symmetric by
construction. The bootstrap distribution assumes neither normality nor
symmetry: it survives a failed or non-positive-definite covariance step, and
it shows asymmetry in poorly identified parameters instead of averaging it
away. Both intervals are returned side by side (\code{ci_lower} / \code{ci_upper} for
the percentile interval, \code{ci_lower_normal} / \code{ci_upper_normal} for the
normal approximation built from the bootstrap standard error) precisely so
the disagreement between them is visible.
}

\subsection{Exclusions}{

A replicate whose minimization terminated, or whose estimate sits on a
boundary, is not a draw from the sampling distribution of a converged fit,
so by default it is excluded from the statistics (PsN's defaults). The
replicate is still fitted and still appears in \verb{$raw}; only the summary
drops it. \verb{$diagnostics} reports how many were dropped and why. To change
the criteria after a run has finished, use \code{\link[=ferx_bootstrap_summarize]{ferx_bootstrap_summarize()}}
instead of refitting - but that needs \code{directory} to have been set.
}

\subsection{Cost}{

This is \code{samples} (+ 1) complete fits. At PsN's rule-of-thumb 200 samples a
model that takes a minute to fit takes over three hours, so \code{threads} is the
argument that matters most. \code{progress} (on by default in an interactive
session) draws a bar as the replicates come in, so a long run says where it
is.

A run is interruptible: Ctrl-C is checked several times a second, the fits
in flight stop at their next iteration, and the call raises \verb{ferx_bootstrap: cancelled by user} rather than returning. It raises rather than returning
the partial run because the replicates the interrupt aborted come back as
\emph{failed} fits, so a returned summary would be a normal-looking table
computed over however many replicates happened to finish. The ones that did
finish are not lost if \code{directory} was set - they are written there as they
land - so set it before starting a long run, then either summarise them with
\code{\link[=ferx_bootstrap_summarize]{ferx_bootstrap_summarize()}} or carry on with \code{resume = TRUE}.
}

\subsection{Resuming an interrupted run}{

\code{resume = TRUE} continues the run in \code{directory}, refitting only the sample
indices its \code{raw_results.csv} does not already carry. It is sound because a
replicate's draw is a pure function of \verb{(seed, index)}: a reused replicate is
bit-for-bit the one an uninterrupted run would have produced, so a resumed
run's artefacts are identical to that run's. The base fit is reused too and
is deliberately \emph{not} refitted - a second fit can land on a slightly
different optimum, and with \code{update_inits = TRUE} that would start the new
replicates from a different point than the ones already on disk.

The engine refuses to resume from a directory that belongs to a different
run: the model and data hashes, the parameter names and the settings that
shape the replicates are recorded alongside them and are checked before any
is reused. Those settings are \code{samples}, \code{seed}, \code{sample_size},
\code{stratify_on}, \code{run_base_model}, \code{update_inits}, \code{keep_covariance} and
\code{dofv} - all of them pinned, so a resumed run cannot \emph{extend} an earlier
one. Calling again with a larger \code{samples} is refused rather than topping
the run up; a bigger bootstrap means a fresh \code{directory}.

A replicate whose fit \emph{errored} is carried forward as a failure rather than
refitted, matching PsN - a fit that failed usually fails again.
\code{retry_failed = TRUE} (which needs \code{resume = TRUE}) refits those instead, and
is for a failure that was transient - an out-of-memory kill, a full disk -
rather than one of the model.
}
